{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Query works by means of hooks - either the ones we offer or custom ones that wrap around them."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Writing unit tests for these custom hooks can be done by means of the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://react-hooks-testing-library.com/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Hooks Testing Library"}]},{"type":"text","value":" library."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install this by running:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install @testing-library/react-hooks react-test-renderer --save-dev","lang":"sh"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"(The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"react-test-renderer"},{"type":"text","value":" library is needed as a peer dependency of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@testing-library/react-hooks"},{"type":"text","value":", and needs to correspond to the version of React that you are using.)"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"our-first-test","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Our First Test"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once installed, a simple test can be written. Given the following custom hook:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export function useCustomHook() {\n  return useQuery('customHook', () => 'Hello');\n}","lang":"js"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can write a test for this as follows:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queryClient = new QueryClient();\nconst wrapper = ({ children }) => (\n  <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n    {children}\n  </QueryClientProvider>\n);\n\nconst { result, waitFor } = renderHook(() => useCustomHook(), { wrapper });\n\nawait waitFor(() => result.current.isSuccess);\n\nexpect(result.current.data).toEqual(\"Hello\");","lang":"js"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note that we provide a custom wrapper that builds the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryClient"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryClientProvider"},{"type":"text","value":". This helps to ensure that our test is completely isolated from any other tests."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is possible to write this wrapper only once, but if so we need to ensure that the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryClient"},{"type":"text","value":" gets cleared before every test, and that tests don't run in parallel otherwise one test will influence the results of others."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"turn-off-retries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Turn off retries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The library defaults to three retries with exponential backoff, which means that your tests are likely to timeout if you want to test an erroneous query. The easiest way to turn retries off is via the QueryClientProvider. Let's extend the above example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queryClient = new QueryClient({\n  defaultOptions: {\n    queries: {\n      // ✅ turns retries off\n      retry: false,\n    },\n  },\n})\nconst wrapper = ({ children }) => (\n  <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n    {children}\n  </QueryClientProvider>\n);","lang":"js"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This will set the defaults for all queries in the component tree to \"no retries\". It is important to know that this will only work if your actual useQuery has no explicit retries set. If you have a query that wants 5 retries, this will still take precedence, because defaults are only taken as a fallback."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"turn-off-network-error-logging","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Turn off network error logging"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When testing we want to suppress network errors being logged to the console.\nTo do this, we can use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"react-query"},{"type":"text","value":"'s "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setLogger()"},{"type":"text","value":" function."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// as part of your test setup\nimport { setLogger } from 'react-query'\n\nsetLogger({\n  log: console.log,\n  warn: console.warn,\n  // ✅ no more errors on the console\n  error: () => {},\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"set-cachetime-to-infinity-with-jest","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set cacheTime to Infinity with Jest"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cacheTime"},{"type":"text","value":" is set to 5 minutes by default. It means that the cache garbage collector timer will be triggered every 5 minutes. If you use Jest, you can set the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cacheTime"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Infinity"},{"type":"text","value":" to prevent \"Jest did not exit one second after the test run completed\" error message."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"testing-network-calls","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Testing Network Calls"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The primary use for React Query is to cache network requests, so it's important that we can test our code is making the correct network requests in the first place."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are plenty of ways that these can be tested, but for this example we are going to use "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.npmjs.com/package/nock","children":[{"type":"text","value":"nock"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Given the following custom hook:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function useFetchData() {\n  return useQuery('fetchData', () => request('/api/data'));\n}","lang":"js"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can write a test for this as follows:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const queryClient = new QueryClient();\nconst wrapper = ({ children }) => (\n  <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n    {children}\n  </QueryClientProvider>\n);\n\nconst expectation = nock('http://example.com')\n  .get('/api/data')\n  .reply(200, {\n    answer: 42\n  });\n\nconst { result, waitFor } = renderHook(() => useFetchData(), { wrapper });\n\nawait waitFor(() => {\n  return result.current.isSuccess;\n});\n\nexpect(result.current.data).toEqual({answer: 42});","lang":"js"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here we are making use of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"waitFor"},{"type":"text","value":" and waiting until the query status indicates that the request has succeeded. This way we know that our hook has finished and should have the correct data."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"testing-load-more-infinite-scroll","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Testing Load More / Infinite Scroll"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"First we need to mock our API response"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function generateMockedResponse(page) {\n  return {\n    page: page,\n    items: [...]\n  }\n}","lang":"js"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Then, our "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nock"},{"type":"text","value":" configuration needs to differentiate responses based on the page, and we'll be using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"uri"},{"type":"text","value":" to do this.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"uri"},{"type":"text","value":"'s value here will be something like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"/?page=1"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/?page=2"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const expectation = nock('http://example.com')\n  .persist()\n  .query(true)\n  .get('/api/data')\n  .reply(200, (uri) => {\n    const url = new URL(`http://example.com${uri}`);\n    const { page } = Object.fromEntries(url.searchParams);\n    return generateMockedResponse(page);\n  });","lang":"js"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"(Notice the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".persist()"},{"type":"text","value":", because we'll be calling from this endpoint multiple times)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now we can safely run our tests, the trick here is to await both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isFetching"},{"type":"text","value":" and then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"!isFetching"},{"type":"text","value":" after calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchNextPage()"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { result, waitFor } = renderHook(() => useInfiniteQueryCustomHook(), { wrapper });\n\nawait waitFor(() => result.current.isSuccess);\n\nexpect(result.current.data.pages).toStrictEqual(generateMockedResponse(1));\n\nresult.current.fetchNextPage();\n\nawait waitFor(() => result.current.isFetching);\nawait waitFor(() => !result.current.isFetching);\n\nexpect(result.current.data.pages).toStrictEqual([\n  ...generateMockedResponse(1),\n  ...generateMockedResponse(2),\n]);\n\nexpectation.done();","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"further-reading","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Further reading"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For additional tips and an alternative setup using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mock-service-worker"},{"type":"text","value":", have a look at "},{"type":"link","href":"../community/tkdodos-blog#5-testing-react-query","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Testing React Query"}]},{"type":"text","value":" from\nthe Community Resources."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"our-first-test","text":"Our First Test","level":2},{"id":"turn-off-retries","text":"Turn off retries","level":2},{"id":"turn-off-network-error-logging","text":"Turn off network error logging","level":2},{"id":"set-cachetime-to-infinity-with-jest","text":"Set cacheTime to Infinity with Jest","level":2},{"id":"testing-network-calls","text":"Testing Network Calls","level":2},{"id":"testing-load-more-infinite-scroll","text":"Testing Load More / Infinite Scroll","level":2},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nReact Query works by means of hooks - either the ones we offer or custom ones that wrap around them.\n\nWriting unit tests for these custom hooks can be done by means of the [React Hooks Testing Library](https://react-hooks-testing-library.com/) library.\n\nInstall this by running:\n\n```sh\nnpm install @testing-library/react-hooks react-test-renderer --save-dev\n```\n\n(The `react-test-renderer` library is needed as a peer dependency of `@testing-library/react-hooks`, and needs to correspond to the version of React that you are using.)\n\n## Our First Test\n\nOnce installed, a simple test can be written. Given the following custom hook:\n\n```js\nexport function useCustomHook() {\n  return useQuery('customHook', () => 'Hello');\n}\n```\n\nWe can write a test for this as follows:\n\n```js\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient();\nconst wrapper = ({ children }) => (\n  <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n    {children}\n  </QueryClientProvider>\n);\n\nconst { result, waitFor } = renderHook(() => useCustomHook(), { wrapper });\n\nawait waitFor(() => result.current.isSuccess);\n\nexpect(result.current.data).toEqual(\"Hello\");\n```\n\nNote that we provide a custom wrapper that builds the `QueryClient` and `QueryClientProvider`. This helps to ensure that our test is completely isolated from any other tests.\n\nIt is possible to write this wrapper only once, but if so we need to ensure that the `QueryClient` gets cleared before every test, and that tests don't run in parallel otherwise one test will influence the results of others.\n\n## Turn off retries\n\nThe library defaults to three retries with exponential backoff, which means that your tests are likely to timeout if you want to test an erroneous query. The easiest way to turn retries off is via the QueryClientProvider. Let's extend the above example:\n\n```js\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient({\n  defaultOptions: {\n    queries: {\n      // ✅ turns retries off\n      retry: false,\n    },\n  },\n})\nconst wrapper = ({ children }) => (\n  <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n    {children}\n  </QueryClientProvider>\n);\n```\n\nThis will set the defaults for all queries in the component tree to \"no retries\". It is important to know that this will only work if your actual useQuery has no explicit retries set. If you have a query that wants 5 retries, this will still take precedence, because defaults are only taken as a fallback.\n\n## Turn off network error logging\n\nWhen testing we want to suppress network errors being logged to the console.\nTo do this, we can use `react-query`'s `setLogger()` function.\n\n```ts\n// as part of your test setup\nimport { setLogger } from 'react-query'\n\nsetLogger({\n  log: console.log,\n  warn: console.warn,\n  // ✅ no more errors on the console\n  error: () => {},\n})\n```\n\n## Set cacheTime to Infinity with Jest\n\n`cacheTime` is set to 5 minutes by default. It means that the cache garbage collector timer will be triggered every 5 minutes. If you use Jest, you can set the `cacheTime` to `Infinity` to prevent \"Jest did not exit one second after the test run completed\" error message.\n\n## Testing Network Calls\n\nThe primary use for React Query is to cache network requests, so it's important that we can test our code is making the correct network requests in the first place.\n\nThere are plenty of ways that these can be tested, but for this example we are going to use [nock](https://www.npmjs.com/package/nock).\n\nGiven the following custom hook:\n\n```js\nfunction useFetchData() {\n  return useQuery('fetchData', () => request('/api/data'));\n}\n```\n\nWe can write a test for this as follows:\n\n```js\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient();\nconst wrapper = ({ children }) => (\n  <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n    {children}\n  </QueryClientProvider>\n);\n\nconst expectation = nock('http://example.com')\n  .get('/api/data')\n  .reply(200, {\n    answer: 42\n  });\n\nconst { result, waitFor } = renderHook(() => useFetchData(), { wrapper });\n\nawait waitFor(() => {\n  return result.current.isSuccess;\n});\n\nexpect(result.current.data).toEqual({answer: 42});\n```\n\nHere we are making use of `waitFor` and waiting until the query status indicates that the request has succeeded. This way we know that our hook has finished and should have the correct data.\n\n## Testing Load More / Infinite Scroll\n\nFirst we need to mock our API response\n\n```js\nfunction generateMockedResponse(page) {\n  return {\n    page: page,\n    items: [...]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThen, our `nock` configuration needs to differentiate responses based on the page, and we'll be using `uri` to do this.\n`uri`'s value here will be something like `\"/?page=1` or `/?page=2`\n\n```js\nconst expectation = nock('http://example.com')\n  .persist()\n  .query(true)\n  .get('/api/data')\n  .reply(200, (uri) => {\n    const url = new URL(`http://example.com${uri}`);\n    const { page } = Object.fromEntries(url.searchParams);\n    return generateMockedResponse(page);\n  });\n```\n\n(Notice the `.persist()`, because we'll be calling from this endpoint multiple times)\n\nNow we can safely run our tests, the trick here is to await both `isFetching` and then `!isFetching` after calling `fetchNextPage()`:\n\n```js\nconst { result, waitFor } = renderHook(() => useInfiniteQueryCustomHook(), { wrapper });\n\nawait waitFor(() => result.current.isSuccess);\n\nexpect(result.current.data.pages).toStrictEqual(generateMockedResponse(1));\n\nresult.current.fetchNextPage();\n\nawait waitFor(() => result.current.isFetching);\nawait waitFor(() => !result.current.isFetching);\n\nexpect(result.current.data.pages).toStrictEqual([\n  ...generateMockedResponse(1),\n  ...generateMockedResponse(2),\n]);\n\nexpectation.done();\n```\n## Further reading\n\nFor additional tips and an alternative setup using `mock-service-worker`, have a look at [Testing React Query](../community/tkdodos-blog#5-testing-react-query) from\nthe Community Resources.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: testing\ntitle: Testing\n---\n\nReact Query works by means of hooks - either the ones we offer or custom ones that wrap around them.\n\nWriting unit tests for these custom hooks can be done by means of the [React Hooks Testing Library](https://react-hooks-testing-library.com/) library.\n\nInstall this by running:\n\n```sh\nnpm install @testing-library/react-hooks react-test-renderer --save-dev\n```\n\n(The `react-test-renderer` library is needed as a peer dependency of `@testing-library/react-hooks`, and needs to correspond to the version of React that you are using.)\n\n## Our First Test\n\nOnce installed, a simple test can be written. Given the following custom hook:\n\n```js\nexport function useCustomHook() {\n  return useQuery('customHook', () => 'Hello');\n}\n```\n\nWe can write a test for this as follows:\n\n```js\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient();\nconst wrapper = ({ children }) => (\n  <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n    {children}\n  </QueryClientProvider>\n);\n\nconst { result, waitFor } = renderHook(() => useCustomHook(), { wrapper });\n\nawait waitFor(() => result.current.isSuccess);\n\nexpect(result.current.data).toEqual(\"Hello\");\n```\n\nNote that we provide a custom wrapper that builds the `QueryClient` and `QueryClientProvider`. This helps to ensure that our test is completely isolated from any other tests.\n\nIt is possible to write this wrapper only once, but if so we need to ensure that the `QueryClient` gets cleared before every test, and that tests don't run in parallel otherwise one test will influence the results of others.\n\n## Turn off retries\n\nThe library defaults to three retries with exponential backoff, which means that your tests are likely to timeout if you want to test an erroneous query. The easiest way to turn retries off is via the QueryClientProvider. Let's extend the above example:\n\n```js\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient({\n  defaultOptions: {\n    queries: {\n      // ✅ turns retries off\n      retry: false,\n    },\n  },\n})\nconst wrapper = ({ children }) => (\n  <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n    {children}\n  </QueryClientProvider>\n);\n```\n\nThis will set the defaults for all queries in the component tree to \"no retries\". It is important to know that this will only work if your actual useQuery has no explicit retries set. If you have a query that wants 5 retries, this will still take precedence, because defaults are only taken as a fallback.\n\n## Turn off network error logging\n\nWhen testing we want to suppress network errors being logged to the console.\nTo do this, we can use `react-query`'s `setLogger()` function.\n\n```ts\n// as part of your test setup\nimport { setLogger } from 'react-query'\n\nsetLogger({\n  log: console.log,\n  warn: console.warn,\n  // ✅ no more errors on the console\n  error: () => {},\n})\n```\n\n## Set cacheTime to Infinity with Jest\n\n`cacheTime` is set to 5 minutes by default. It means that the cache garbage collector timer will be triggered every 5 minutes. If you use Jest, you can set the `cacheTime` to `Infinity` to prevent \"Jest did not exit one second after the test run completed\" error message.\n\n## Testing Network Calls\n\nThe primary use for React Query is to cache network requests, so it's important that we can test our code is making the correct network requests in the first place.\n\nThere are plenty of ways that these can be tested, but for this example we are going to use [nock](https://www.npmjs.com/package/nock).\n\nGiven the following custom hook:\n\n```js\nfunction useFetchData() {\n  return useQuery('fetchData', () => request('/api/data'));\n}\n```\n\nWe can write a test for this as follows:\n\n```js\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient();\nconst wrapper = ({ children }) => (\n  <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n    {children}\n  </QueryClientProvider>\n);\n\nconst expectation = nock('http://example.com')\n  .get('/api/data')\n  .reply(200, {\n    answer: 42\n  });\n\nconst { result, waitFor } = renderHook(() => useFetchData(), { wrapper });\n\nawait waitFor(() => {\n  return result.current.isSuccess;\n});\n\nexpect(result.current.data).toEqual({answer: 42});\n```\n\nHere we are making use of `waitFor` and waiting until the query status indicates that the request has succeeded. This way we know that our hook has finished and should have the correct data.\n\n## Testing Load More / Infinite Scroll\n\nFirst we need to mock our API response\n\n```js\nfunction generateMockedResponse(page) {\n  return {\n    page: page,\n    items: [...]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThen, our `nock` configuration needs to differentiate responses based on the page, and we'll be using `uri` to do this.\n`uri`'s value here will be something like `\"/?page=1` or `/?page=2`\n\n```js\nconst expectation = nock('http://example.com')\n  .persist()\n  .query(true)\n  .get('/api/data')\n  .reply(200, (uri) => {\n    const url = new URL(`http://example.com${uri}`);\n    const { page } = Object.fromEntries(url.searchParams);\n    return generateMockedResponse(page);\n  });\n```\n\n(Notice the `.persist()`, because we'll be calling from this endpoint multiple times)\n\nNow we can safely run our tests, the trick here is to await both `isFetching` and then `!isFetching` after calling `fetchNextPage()`:\n\n```js\nconst { result, waitFor } = renderHook(() => useInfiniteQueryCustomHook(), { wrapper });\n\nawait waitFor(() => result.current.isSuccess);\n\nexpect(result.current.data.pages).toStrictEqual(generateMockedResponse(1));\n\nresult.current.fetchNextPage();\n\nawait waitFor(() => result.current.isFetching);\nawait waitFor(() => !result.current.isFetching);\n\nexpect(result.current.data.pages).toStrictEqual([\n  ...generateMockedResponse(1),\n  ...generateMockedResponse(2),\n]);\n\nexpectation.done();\n```\n## Further reading\n\nFor additional tips and an alternative setup using `mock-service-worker`, have a look at [Testing React Query](../community/tkdodos-blog#5-testing-react-query) from\nthe Community Resources.\n"}
